WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.495
RAII connects resource management to object lifetime.

2
00:00:02.495 --> 00:00:06.773
When an owning object is constructed, it acquires or represents a resource.

3
00:00:06.773 --> 00:00:10.695
When that owning object is destroyed, its destructor releases the resource.

4
00:00:10.695 --> 00:00:16.399
This pattern makes cleanup predictable and reduces the need for manual cleanup scattered across every branch.

5
00:00:16.399 --> 00:00:18.181
std::unique_ptr expresses exclusive ownership.

6
00:00:18.181 --> 00:00:24.955
It is a good fit when one object or scope is responsible for keeping a dynamically allocated object alive.

7
00:00:24.955 --> 00:00:27.807
Moving a unique pointer transfers that responsibility visibly.

8
00:00:27.807 --> 00:00:31.015
That makes ownership changes easier to see during review.

9
00:00:31.015 --> 00:00:32.798
std::shared_ptr expresses shared ownership.

10
00:00:32.798 --> 00:00:38.145
It is appropriate when multiple owners truly need to keep the same object alive independently.

11
00:00:38.145 --> 00:00:42.423
The tradeoff is that shared ownership can make destruction timing less obvious.

12
00:00:42.423 --> 00:00:47.771
If it is used casually, it may hide a design question rather than answer it.

13
00:00:47.771 --> 00:00:52.405
std::weak_ptr supports non-owning observation of an object managed by shared ownership.

14
00:00:52.405 --> 00:00:58.465
It lets code check whether the object is still available before use without extending lifetime by itself.

15
00:00:58.465 --> 00:01:03.813
This is useful for caches, callbacks, registries, and observer relationships where ownership should remain elsewhere.

16
00:01:03.813 --> 00:01:06.665
Smart pointers are not a substitute for design.

17
00:01:06.665 --> 00:01:08.804
They should reflect real ownership relationships.

18
00:01:08.804 --> 00:01:13.795
Replacing every raw pointer with shared ownership can make a system harder to understand.

19
00:01:13.795 --> 00:01:22.707
The right question is not which smart pointer looks safer, but who should own the object and how long observers are allowed to use it.

20
00:01:22.707 --> 00:01:25.916
Manual delete paths should be minimized and reviewed carefully.

21
00:01:25.916 --> 00:01:32.689
When manual cleanup remains, the code should have clear single ownership, predictable failure behavior, and tests around destruction order.

22
00:01:32.689 --> 00:01:37.680
RAII wrappers can often isolate older cleanup patterns while the surrounding design is modernized.
